WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:04.770
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.070
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:07.370
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:08.610
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.430
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:19.410
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:20.550
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:25.700
Hello, everyone.

9
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:26.920
Howdy.

10
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:31.500
Today, we are breaking down Goodbye, Toby, Part 1.

11
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.260
I can't believe it.

12
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.420
We're at the end of Season 4.

13
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:36.280
I can't believe it, Jenna.

14
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.600
I'm excited, though.

15
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:38.860
This is such a good episode.

16
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:40.180
Oh, I loved it.

17
00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:41.680
Beyond loved it.

18
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:50.860
It is Season 4, Episode 18, written by Jen Salata and Paul Lieberstein and directed by Paul Feig.

19
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.180
Hashtag beyond loved.

20
00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:55.800
Yeah, beyond loved.

21
00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.680
Well, I know you've got a great summary and some fast facts.

22
00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:00.800
I can't wait to hear them.

23
00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:01.880
Let's get started.

24
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.700
Toby is moving to Costa Rica.

25
00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.080
Finally, his lifelong dream is coming true.

26
00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:13.460
Michael insists the party planning committee plan a huge going-away party.

27
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.340
And when Angela balks, he puts Phyllis in charge.

28
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:18.100
Ugh.

29
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.320
Can you believe it?

30
00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.680
I mean, she does a really good job.

31
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:23.620
You know what?

32
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:24.620
I don't need to hear it.

33
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.760
Well, I'm going to talk about it more later.

34
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:27.800
Well, okay.

35
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.580
There is a new HR representative coming in, Holly Flax.

36
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.880
She will be taking over for Toby, and this is her first day at Dunder Mifflin.

37
00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:41.200
Michael assumes he will hate her, but he is quickly smitten.

38
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:42.740
Very quickly.

39
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:43.980
Very quickly.

40
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.240
But that is Michael.

41
00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.280
I mean, Michael falls in love at Hello.

42
00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:48.500
He does.

43
00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.120
Meanwhile, Pam gets some good news.

44
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.460
Should we get started?

45
00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.780
Fast fact number one.

46
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.160
I thought you were going to tip your hand a little bit.

47
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.940
Is it tip your hand or tip your hat?

48
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:02.900
It's tip your hat.

49
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:07.040
No, I think it's your hand because you're playing poker and you tip it and people see

50
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:07.620
your cards.

51
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:08.460
Right.

52
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.640
But you tip your hat for something else.

53
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:11.240
As a hello?

54
00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:11.940
Maybe.

55
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.940
But I thought you were going to hint at what Pam's big news was.

56
00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:18.480
But no, let's get into some fast facts.

57
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:19.200
All right.

58
00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.560
Let's not tip anything.

59
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.440
We're tipping nothing right now.

60
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:23.100
Tip nothing.

61
00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.040
Fast fact number one.

62
00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.840
I find it very funny that Paul had to write himself out of the show.

63
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.900
I thought it was funny, too.

64
00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:34.900
But knowing Paul, because he's very practical, it was probably like, well, it needs to be done.

65
00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.520
There is a very practical reason for why he wrote himself out of the show that we know.

66
00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:45.440
Paul and Jen are going to take over as the showrunners of The Office starting in season

67
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:49.540
five because Greg was going to be working on Parks and Recreation.

68
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:50.260
Yep.

69
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:51.940
Now, Greg was still around.

70
00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:53.700
He had a foot in both worlds.

71
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.500
Yeah, he'd pop by, but he wasn't going to be there day to day.

72
00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.400
That was Jen and Paul.

73
00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:03.400
So they teamed up to write this episode and they were really trying to set themselves up

74
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.160
for next season.

75
00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.880
Paul needed a break from the acting.

76
00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.280
He wanted to concentrate on writing the show.

77
00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:12.440
We traded emails with Paul and he sent in an audio clip all about it.

78
00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:18.580
Jen Salata and I were taking over as showrunners for season five, and that was absolutely part

79
00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:19.660
of getting rid of me.

80
00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:22.960
Give me some time to just showrun and concentrate on that.

81
00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.740
Did we know for a fact I'd be coming back?

82
00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.120
No, not for a fact.

83
00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.480
Didn't really think about it that far in.

84
00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.240
There was a lot of things started.

85
00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.260
Pam's acceptance to art school.

86
00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.520
That was something we had talked about for a long time.

87
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:44.620
We always knew she liked it, but we were looking for, you know, a whole new complication after

88
00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.460
doing so much with the romance.

89
00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.780
And then Angela's engagement to Andy.

90
00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:53.680
We always knew that was not a relationship to be.

91
00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.780
That was a bad relationship, a comedy relationship.

92
00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.680
But it gave us some fun stuff, obviously.

93
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:04.640
There's so many big storylines that they set up with this finale.

94
00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:05.840
I know.

95
00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.420
Oh, I mean, we haven't even talked about Jan.

96
00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:09.520
That's part two.

97
00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:10.620
But my goodness.

98
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:11.620
Oh, I know.

99
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:12.660
Big, big.

100
00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:13.640
What do they call it?

101
00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.000
The cliffhanger.

102
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:16.960
Big cliffhanger.

103
00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.520
Why am I having a hard time with like common phrases today?

104
00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:24.180
Buckle in, everybody, because this might be a bumpy ride.

105
00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:25.460
Well, I'm here for it.

106
00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:27.920
I love when you mangle common phrases.

107
00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.760
It's one of my favorite things about you.

108
00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:30.800
I don't know why.

109
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.000
I almost said it's like the cliff's edge.

110
00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:36.000
And then in my brain, I was like, no, no, no.

111
00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:36.560
That's not right.

112
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:37.540
You'll find it, lady.

113
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:39.460
All right.

114
00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:40.000
Are you ready?

115
00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:41.300
Fast fact number two.

116
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:41.900
Yeah.

117
00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.480
Jen Salata said that this script took about five days to write.

118
00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:47.600
Five days.

119
00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:48.720
That's crazy.

120
00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:50.800
How did they do it in five days?

121
00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.460
It would take me so long to write this.

122
00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:53.840
This is a juggernaut.

123
00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:54.540
I know.

124
00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.860
She said they had an outline and they divided up the work and they each wrote different parts

125
00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:03.040
and then they put it all together and they did a rewrite together.

126
00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:06.080
So, I mean, I guess there were two people on it, but still.

127
00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:14.500
Also, the original cut of this episode came in at 72 minutes and they had to cut it down

128
00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:15.400
to 40 minutes.

129
00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:19.860
Lady, guess who found her shooting draft of Goodbye, Toby?

130
00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.140
You, electronic hoarder.

131
00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:25.260
Yes, digital hoarder, Angela Kinsey.

132
00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:31.600
All right, Jenna, since I have the shooting draft of Goodbye, Toby, I have a little quiz

133
00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:32.140
for you.

134
00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.480
Oh, all right.

135
00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:36.640
You're like, I didn't know I was going to get tested today.

136
00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:37.300
Okay.

137
00:05:37.620 --> 00:05:42.340
Can you guess how many pages were in the shooting draft?

138
00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:45.040
Oh, well, let me think.

139
00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:51.140
If I remember correctly, our standard shooting drafts were usually around 30 pages.

140
00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:52.140
Yep.

141
00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:56.560
So, since this was a double episode, I'm going to say 60 pages?

142
00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:57.900
75!

143
00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:00.460
Oh, my gosh.

144
00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.280
Well, no wonder the original cut came in at 72 minutes.

145
00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:05.260
I know!

146
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.860
They always say each page of a script equals one minute of screen time.

147
00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.320
On average, right?

148
00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.820
Well, lady, there was so much on the page that did not make it in this episode.

149
00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:18.760
Well, I'm guessing there are a lot of deleted scenes.

150
00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:22.020
Oh, there's some wonderful deleted scenes.

151
00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.680
And then there's even more scenes that aren't in the deleted scenes.

152
00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:27.140
I really enjoyed this.

153
00:06:27.260 --> 00:06:28.760
I'm going to sprinkle things throughout.

154
00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.720
And you know what it really made me respect, Jenna?

155
00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:32.480
What?

156
00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:33.500
Our editors.

157
00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:34.820
I'll tell you why.

158
00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:37.820
I re-watched this episode four times.

159
00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:42.220
And one of the times I re-watched it, I just followed along in the script.

160
00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.960
And the scenes were moved all around in editing.

161
00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.020
I was fascinated by it.

162
00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:52.660
And it really just shows you what storytellers our editors are.

163
00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.720
You know, I remember that, Angela.

164
00:06:54.940 --> 00:07:01.700
I remember that they would almost, like, rewrite the script again in the editing process.

165
00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:02.100
Yeah.

166
00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:03.460
Because they would do that.

167
00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:07.220
They would move scenes around from where they had been placed in the script originally.

168
00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:08.520
I love that.

169
00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.540
Well, are you ready for fast fact number three?

170
00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:12.320
Yeah.

171
00:07:12.780 --> 00:07:14.200
Fast fact number three.

172
00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.800
Amy Ryan, a.k.a.

173
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.060
Holly Flax, a.k.a.

174
00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.640
Michael's True Love.

175
00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:21.640
Aww.

176
00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:24.040
I loved Holly Flax.

177
00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:25.020
I loved her.

178
00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:28.900
We had a fan question from Zianna G.

179
00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:34.360
Was Amy Ryan asked to play Holly or did she have to go through the audition process?

180
00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.980
Amy did not have to audition for the role of Holly Flax.

181
00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:39.580
It was offered to her.

182
00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:40.900
And this is why.

183
00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:46.340
First of all, Amy Ryan had just been nominated for an Academy Award.

184
00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:49.860
We had another Academy Award nominee on our show.

185
00:07:50.060 --> 00:07:53.960
She'd been nominated for a movie called Gone Baby Gone.

186
00:07:54.260 --> 00:08:01.820
And in addition to that, the writing staff of The Office was slightly obsessed with the TV show The Wire.

187
00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:04.300
And Amy had been on The Wire.

188
00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:11.700
And whenever we had, like, big guest star roles come up, the writers would try to get cast members from The Wire to do them.

189
00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.520
I mean, spoiler alert, Idris Elba is coming up.

190
00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:15.200
Mm-hmm.

191
00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:17.800
But there is actually even more to this story.

192
00:08:17.960 --> 00:08:20.220
But we will let Paul Lieberstein tell you.

193
00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.540
Here's an audio clip from Paul where he also talks about their overall plans for the role of Holly.

194
00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:29.900
Amy Ryan, her agents were asking her, well, what do you want to do?

195
00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:31.460
And she said, I want to do The Office.

196
00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:35.200
And at the very same time, we were talking about her.

197
00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.340
And I don't really know who reached out to who firSt.

198
00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:44.240
I worked with Amy like a million years ago on another show.

199
00:08:44.240 --> 00:08:45.400
And we knew each other.

200
00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:48.120
And I was all for it.

201
00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.920
I loved working with her both times.

202
00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.880
So we didn't know how much of an arc was it going to be with Amy.

203
00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:54.820
We didn't really know.

204
00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.260
We didn't have this kind of long-term deal.

205
00:08:57.260 --> 00:09:02.600
We wanted to kind of test it out and see because you never know about chemistry, right?

206
00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:05.260
You never know if she's going to have chemistry with Steve.

207
00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:06.640
And she sure did.

208
00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:12.800
I don't think we knew that this was going to be the, you know, love of his life.

209
00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:15.100
We hadn't planned it that far ahead.

210
00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.360
We were also watching what happened and reacting to it.

211
00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.380
Can you believe that she said The Office?

212
00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:23.360
I was so tickled by that.

213
00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:28.600
I mean, she did this interview where she said she had been doing these heavy dramas.

214
00:09:29.020 --> 00:09:31.300
And she really wanted to lighten things up.

215
00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:32.620
She wanted to do a comedy.

216
00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:34.220
And she loved our show.

217
00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:37.000
So that's what she told her agent she wanted to do.

218
00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.120
Here are her crossover The Office connections.

219
00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.180
Jenna, she was not on Monk.

220
00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:45.420
Oh, no, she wasn't on Monk.

221
00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:49.380
But the show that her and Paul worked on was in the 90s.

222
00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:52.300
And it was called The Naked Truth with Taya Leone.

223
00:09:52.540 --> 00:09:54.700
And Paul Lieberstein was a writer on it.

224
00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:55.780
So that's where they met.

225
00:09:55.980 --> 00:10:01.960
And then she and Steve Carell did a movie together in 2007 called Dan in Real Life.

226
00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:03.520
So they had worked together before.

227
00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:04.440
Yes.

228
00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:10.240
Kentapedia told me that they had locked her down for six episodes for season five

229
00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:11.900
when she did this guest role.

230
00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:13.440
But that's as far as they went.

231
00:10:13.660 --> 00:10:17.000
And kind of like what Paul said, they didn't know if this was going to be

232
00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:19.300
a temporary love interest for Michael.

233
00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:20.460
They weren't sure.

234
00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:24.700
But they did have her set to start off the next season.

235
00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:28.980
There are so many great scenes between Holly and Michael in this episode.

236
00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.560
And there are even more wonderful ones in the script.

237
00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:33.720
I really wish they all could have stayed in.

238
00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.000
I know they had to trim them.

239
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:40.860
But you really see the build of their sort of mutual, like, crush on each other.

240
00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.520
I got choked up watching this episode.

241
00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:50.160
Every time she returns his affection or flirts with him a little bit.

242
00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:56.320
I mean, I was so excited for Michael that a woman was being kind to him.

243
00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:57.920
I mean, I was emotional.

244
00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.340
Not just kind, but she gets him.

245
00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:01.780
They have the same humor.

246
00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:02.780
Yes.

247
00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:04.500
Well, lady, that's all I got.

248
00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:05.440
Should we take a break?

249
00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:07.680
And then we'll come back and get into this episode.

250
00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:08.660
Let's do it.

251
00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:19.490
All right.

252
00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:22.790
We are back with a classic gym prank.

253
00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:24.850
This is our cold open.

254
00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:26.490
And Michael is not in it.

255
00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:27.170
Did you notice?

256
00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:28.330
I did notice.

257
00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:29.890
His door is shut.

258
00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:33.530
We're being led to believe that Steve is behind that door, but he was not.

259
00:11:33.730 --> 00:11:35.790
He was probably prepping for a film.

260
00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:39.150
Or maybe finally getting to sleep in for a second.

261
00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:39.830
Oh, my gosh.

262
00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:41.010
Having the morning off.

263
00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:42.450
I hope he had the morning off.

264
00:11:43.590 --> 00:11:44.910
Dwight's cell phone rings.

265
00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:49.650
And somehow Jim answers it on his little Bluetooth thing and his, you know, his ear.

266
00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:51.390
Do you remember those?

267
00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:52.130
Yes.

268
00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:53.730
And people would walk around with them.

269
00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:54.430
Yeah.

270
00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:58.110
I mean, we kind of still have this like earbud thing that we do.

271
00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:01.710
But back in the day, y'all, those things were giant.

272
00:12:01.910 --> 00:12:03.930
Did you see how big that thing was on Jim's ear?

273
00:12:04.090 --> 00:12:05.490
This is an old tech alert.

274
00:12:05.770 --> 00:12:06.390
For sure.

275
00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.090
And it is starting to drive Dwight crazy.

276
00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:11.490
Why are all of his calls going to Jim?

277
00:12:11.750 --> 00:12:15.310
Jim explains that Dwight left his cell phone on his desk.

278
00:12:15.510 --> 00:12:18.090
So naturally, he paired it to his headset.

279
00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:19.710
But it goes on.

280
00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:20.990
Dwight's like, fine.

281
00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.970
Then I'm going to reprogram my cell phone to go to my desk.

282
00:12:24.310 --> 00:12:26.210
Well, Jim took care of that, too.

283
00:12:26.390 --> 00:12:28.730
He forwarded Dwight's desk phone to his desk phone.

284
00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:39.790
You know, I'm probably going to get some hate for this, but it does make me feel like Ryan was correct in sort of calling Jim out on his productivity.

285
00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:44.190
Because look at all the effort he went into this whole like earpiece prank.

286
00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:46.970
And he probably could have made some sales during this time.

287
00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:51.670
Here's what I want to say about that, because I worked in corporate America for a long time.

288
00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:59.190
And I often felt like I could get all of my work done in about five hours, but I was required to sit there for eight.

289
00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:06.150
I mean, sometimes you can get what needs to be done faster than the company has allotted.

290
00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:08.810
And so I think that was the case with Jim.

291
00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:10.750
I think he does very good in sales.

292
00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:11.550
I don't know.

293
00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:15.530
I guess Ryan was like, what if you really spent those extra hours making sales?

294
00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:16.690
But I don't know.

295
00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:17.770
He's getting it done.

296
00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:23.450
And he's, I mean, sometimes I feel like these work hours are rather arbitrary.

297
00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:26.990
How about work until your work is done and then go home and enjoy your life?

298
00:13:27.230 --> 00:13:28.210
I like that.

299
00:13:28.550 --> 00:13:32.330
Jenna, you sound like some upstart company where you're like, here's the thing.

300
00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:33.670
Everyone gets pockets.

301
00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:36.490
Yes, you work as long as you want.

302
00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:37.630
Get your s*** done.

303
00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:38.530
We don't care.

304
00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:46.610
Also, we have a soapbox in the lobby and you can stand on it at any point in your day if you need to make any declarations about anything.

305
00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:52.230
Yeah, and finally, we have like a zombie apocalypse shelter ready to go.

306
00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:54.610
Yeah, we have a panic room totally stocked.

307
00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:55.790
That's Jenna's company.

308
00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:57.090
Who wants to work for me?

309
00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:58.290
Come work for me.

310
00:13:58.450 --> 00:13:59.370
That sounds amazing.

311
00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:00.910
You know what is funny?

312
00:14:00.950 --> 00:14:04.070
I've basically created that job with this podcaSt.

313
00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:05.090
You have.

314
00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:08.810
You've given yourself all of those things, except maybe the zombie apocalypse room.

315
00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:09.350
I don't know.

316
00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:11.190
Do you have a, wait, is there something you need to tell me?

317
00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:14.410
I don't have a whole room, but I've got all my backpacks ready to go.

318
00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:18.970
I have a catch for this cold open.

319
00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:20.250
It's a Jenna catch.

320
00:14:20.490 --> 00:14:24.190
During this cold open, Jim pretends to talk to Dwight's mother.

321
00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:24.770
Yeah.

322
00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:25.150
Right?

323
00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:25.830
He says,

324
00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:28.250
Mutter, tell Vater I'm getting married.

325
00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:29.170
Yeah.

326
00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:36.790
Well, in Fun Run, do you remember, Angela, when Dwight is trying to make your character feel better about Sprinkles dying?

327
00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:45.730
He offers to have Sprinkles stuffed, or he offers to have Sprinkles buried in the East Field by mother.

328
00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:46.490
Yeah.

329
00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:48.290
Dwight's mother is not living.

330
00:14:48.710 --> 00:14:49.010
Yeah.

331
00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:51.450
Little continuity error.

332
00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:54.030
Yes, I was very proud of my catch.

333
00:14:54.510 --> 00:14:56.070
Well done, Jenna.

334
00:14:56.710 --> 00:14:57.630
Guess what?

335
00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:58.190
What?

336
00:14:58.510 --> 00:14:59.990
It's Toby's last day.

337
00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:02.250
Michael is dancing through the office.

338
00:15:02.430 --> 00:15:03.750
He's gotten there so early.

339
00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:06.010
No one is there, and he's literally dancing.

340
00:15:06.010 --> 00:15:11.450
I mean, he says, today is one of those days you know you will remember for the rest of your life.

341
00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:12.130
I know.

342
00:15:12.350 --> 00:15:16.290
It's a countdown until his horribleness is out of the building.

343
00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:24.190
He sets an alarm on his watch that's going to go off as Toby has his final moments in the office.

344
00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:25.250
He's so excited.

345
00:15:25.430 --> 00:15:25.990
He's giddy.

346
00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:27.290
Toby arrives.

347
00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:31.030
He goes back to HR, and Kelly hugs him.

348
00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:32.510
She can't believe it's his last day.

349
00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:33.590
She feels so weird.

350
00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:35.170
Toby feels fine.

351
00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:36.430
He's not upset.

352
00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:37.090
Yeah.

353
00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:38.150
All right.

354
00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:39.870
Conference room meeting, Angela.

355
00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:43.950
The party planning committee is going to discuss Toby's goodbye party.

356
00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:46.230
I loved every minute of this scene.

357
00:15:46.370 --> 00:15:48.190
I loved every minute of doing it.

358
00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:49.750
I loved every minute of watching it.

359
00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:52.230
I am so very pregnant.

360
00:15:52.230 --> 00:15:55.530
I am at that stage in pregnancy where you can't hide it.

361
00:15:55.590 --> 00:15:56.390
Look at my face.

362
00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:59.090
No, your belly is practically in your neck.

363
00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:00.270
Let's be honeSt.

364
00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:02.230
I don't know how you fit under that table.

365
00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:04.650
I'm a short person, you guys.

366
00:16:05.030 --> 00:16:07.130
Tall people, when they're pregnant, they go up.

367
00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:09.930
But when you're short, you just go out.

368
00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:13.470
And it was like my head was sitting on top of my belly.

369
00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:17.010
Angela tells Michael, here's what they've got for the party.

370
00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:22.370
A buttercream cake and a slideshow of Toby, but they only have two photos of Toby.

371
00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:27.050
They're just two different expressions standing getting coffee in the kitchen.

372
00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:27.850
It's amazing.

373
00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:30.550
Michael is like, absolutely not.

374
00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:31.170
No, no, no.

375
00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:32.890
This needs to be very special.

376
00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:35.370
Toby is going away forever.

377
00:16:35.810 --> 00:16:38.710
He wants the party to end all parties.

378
00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:39.390
Yeah.

379
00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:44.190
He says when someone in New Orleans leaves, they throw a parade for them.

380
00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:48.750
And Pam says, you want to throw Toby a New Orleans funeral?

381
00:16:49.050 --> 00:16:50.590
Because that's what that means.

382
00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:52.670
That's the parade Michael is talking about.

383
00:16:52.670 --> 00:16:57.350
In the shooting draft, Jenna, there were a few more extra lines to the scene that really

384
00:16:57.350 --> 00:16:58.050
tickled me.

385
00:16:58.390 --> 00:16:59.990
So Pam says that line.

386
00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:02.890
And then Michael says, if that's appropriate, then that's what I want.

387
00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:05.210
I want something that says, oh, wow.

388
00:17:05.570 --> 00:17:06.090
Okay.

389
00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:07.250
Oh, God.

390
00:17:07.470 --> 00:17:08.650
Thank God.

391
00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:12.190
And then Angela says, a prayer?

392
00:17:12.590 --> 00:17:14.070
You want a prayer party?

393
00:17:14.570 --> 00:17:20.290
And then that's when Michael says, if the devil were to explode and evil disappeared, that's what

394
00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:23.090
says in the script, that's the kind of party I want to throw.

395
00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:28.930
Angela explains they only have enough money for a cake and maybe to develop a few more

396
00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:29.470
slides.

397
00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:30.990
Toby will not be in them.

398
00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:34.110
And that is when Michael gets out his shoe.

399
00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:35.110
As one does.

400
00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:39.950
You get out your shoe, you pull up the insole, and you have a wad of cash.

401
00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:40.810
Yeah.

402
00:17:41.270 --> 00:17:44.950
Michael explains every year his grandma sends him $50 for his birthday.

403
00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:48.770
But lately, she's been sending him about nine or 10 checks a year.

404
00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:53.910
He was saving for a motorcycle, Angela, but he thinks this is what he wants to spend his

405
00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:54.370
money on.

406
00:17:54.590 --> 00:17:57.010
We had a fan question from Isabella D.

407
00:17:57.390 --> 00:18:03.350
In Catcher in the Rye, Holden says that his loaded grandmother has been losing her marbles

408
00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:07.870
a little bit and has been sending him money for his birthday multiple times a year.

409
00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:11.750
Was this a happy coincidence or an intentional reference?

410
00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:13.430
And who came up with it?

411
00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:14.650
I don't know.

412
00:18:15.050 --> 00:18:15.830
Do you know?

413
00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:17.070
I found out.

414
00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:18.510
Paul wrote this.

415
00:18:18.690 --> 00:18:20.750
He said it was not intentional.

416
00:18:20.770 --> 00:18:22.530
It's just a happy coincidence.

417
00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:25.210
But I would like to say, Isabella D.

418
00:18:25.690 --> 00:18:26.330
Applause.

419
00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:29.810
I applaud you for being very, very well read.

420
00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:30.510
Yes.

421
00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:33.570
And bringing up this literary parallel reference.

422
00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:34.630
Yeah.

423
00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:39.370
Well, Michael starts listing all of these ridiculous things he wants at this party.

424
00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:44.910
He says he wants to feel like he's flying through the air without this ugly weight holding him

425
00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:45.190
down.

426
00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:47.270
He wants an anti-gravity machine.

427
00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:48.290
And you know what?

428
00:18:48.530 --> 00:18:49.610
Angela has had it.

429
00:18:49.850 --> 00:18:50.590
She has had it.

430
00:18:50.650 --> 00:18:52.310
She said she doesn't want his foot money.

431
00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:54.110
Oh, that made me laugh out loud, lady.

432
00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:55.710
I loved foot money.

433
00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:57.510
I mean, I laughed out loud.

434
00:18:57.510 --> 00:19:02.370
You know, in the script, Jenna, I looked because I was curious about the direction of the scene.

435
00:19:02.590 --> 00:19:06.010
And it says that Angela pushes the money back towards Michael.

436
00:19:06.390 --> 00:19:09.190
And I added that I did it with my pen.

437
00:19:09.390 --> 00:19:10.050
That was me.

438
00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:11.470
That's what I loved about it.

439
00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:13.030
You didn't even want to touch it.

440
00:19:13.270 --> 00:19:13.450
No.

441
00:19:13.570 --> 00:19:15.190
It disgusted you so much.

442
00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:15.770
Yeah.

443
00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:17.190
But it was so fun.

444
00:19:17.350 --> 00:19:19.830
I remember us getting really tickled, Jenna.

445
00:19:19.830 --> 00:19:20.790
Oh, my gosh.

446
00:19:20.910 --> 00:19:24.090
We loved these scenes with Steve and the party planning committee.

447
00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:26.330
They are truly some of my favorite to shoot.

448
00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:27.770
Yeah, it was just candy.

449
00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:28.770
It didn't feel like work.

450
00:19:28.770 --> 00:19:29.950
I just couldn't wait.

451
00:19:29.950 --> 00:19:34.070
And my biggest goal was just to not laugh so that we could get some usable footage.

452
00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:37.590
Well, now Michael is going to put Phyllis in charge.

453
00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:40.390
I loved it so much.

454
00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:47.590
Phyllis crushed it the way she said yes when Michael asked her if she will plan the party.

455
00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:51.830
It is like she has waited for this moment her whole life.

456
00:19:52.110 --> 00:19:54.650
So I have a little script tidbit to share.

457
00:19:54.870 --> 00:20:00.490
After Phyllis says yes, it would have cut immediately to an Angela talking head.

458
00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:05.270
And it says Angela's talking head just starts with a huge fake laugh.

459
00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:13.540
And then Angela says, I was going to let Phyllis be in charge of cups today.

460
00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:18.620
And even then, I had a stack of cups standing by in my trunk.

461
00:20:19.960 --> 00:20:20.980
Of course you did.

462
00:20:22.100 --> 00:20:23.400
Isn't that so good?

463
00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:27.580
They must have just loved writing those snarky talking heads for you, Angela.

464
00:20:27.740 --> 00:20:28.420
They're so good.

465
00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:30.740
I was going to let her be in charge of cups.

466
00:20:30.940 --> 00:20:31.340
Sort of.

467
00:20:31.480 --> 00:20:32.800
But I doubt she could handle it.

468
00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:34.320
Well, now the big moment.

469
00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:37.600
Holly Flax arrives.

470
00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:39.120
New HR rep.

471
00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:47.380
Yes, and Toby has a talking head that as part of his last day, he's going to be training the new human resources rep.

472
00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:51.900
And he says, again, the company has allotted only one day for this.

473
00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:53.280
One training day.

474
00:20:53.400 --> 00:20:54.060
That's all you need.

475
00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:55.200
That's it?

476
00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:55.980
Yeah.

477
00:20:56.500 --> 00:20:59.080
That's all you're going to say about this moment.

478
00:20:59.120 --> 00:20:59.520
What?

479
00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:02.500
What are you hinting at?

480
00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:05.000
I can't believe I have to bring this up.

481
00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:05.960
Lady.

482
00:21:06.280 --> 00:21:06.560
What?

483
00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:09.300
The blooming plant at reception?

484
00:21:09.940 --> 00:21:10.680
Hold up.

485
00:21:10.960 --> 00:21:12.740
With your shade, Jenna Fisher.

486
00:21:13.420 --> 00:21:16.540
I have a whole plant breakdown I was going to bring up later.

487
00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:17.380
Thank you very much.

488
00:21:17.520 --> 00:21:19.380
I was going to let Holly have her moment.

489
00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:20.440
Oh.

490
00:21:20.820 --> 00:21:21.400
All right.

491
00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:22.840
Well, I shouldn't have doubted you.

492
00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:23.380
I'm sorry.

493
00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:24.200
Don't doubt me.

494
00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:27.380
But I mean, all I saw was a blooming plant at reception.

495
00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:29.840
She's carrying a plant in her box.

496
00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.760
It was just like a plant explosion.

497
00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:36.360
And as I was watching this episode, I thought, well, Angela is just going to love that.

498
00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:37.260
So many plants.

499
00:21:37.540 --> 00:21:40.100
I did not notice the plant in her box.

500
00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:41.640
She has a plant in her box?

501
00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:43.460
She has a plant in her box.

502
00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:46.760
How many times are we going to say plant in her box?

503
00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:48.860
Hashtag plant in her box.

504
00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:49.780
It sounds dirty.

505
00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:50.820
Is that just me?

506
00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:52.700
No, it's not just you.

507
00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:54.540
But I kind of like it.

508
00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:56.220
I like it.

509
00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:58.280
Be like, hey, how was last night?

510
00:21:58.420 --> 00:21:59.720
Did you get a plant in your box?

511
00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:01.400
Is that too much?

512
00:22:01.560 --> 00:22:05.020
I want you to know if you go down there, I have a plant in my box.

513
00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:06.580
Oh, my gosh.

514
00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:08.800
What are we even talking about right now?

515
00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:10.400
We don't even know what we're talking about.

516
00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:11.640
